--| The test host: it runs one test of a program's, as `nanyx test` does for each.

  A test build's entry point takes the index of a test and runs it (see the Test builds page of the
  docs). Each test runs in a fresh instance of the build: the runtime starts one, and `run` asks it,
  through `nanyx:test`, an import of the `nanyx` runtime's own, which test to run and with what
  seed, and runs it. A test passes if the instance finishes, and fails if it crashes, so a test that
  fails ends only its own instance.

  The host provides [`Console`], whose output it gives the runtime, which shows it only for a test
  that fails, and whose input is empty; [`Random`], seeded with the runtime's seed, as
  [`random.seeded`] seeds it, so a test that draws random numbers draws the same ones each time it
  runs with that seed; and `Async`, so a test waits as a program does, on a real clock. A test marked
  `@test(#io)` has the console host's real `Console`, `Env` and `FileSystem` instead, in the
  package's directory, and its output is still kept. A test loads any other context it needs with
  `use`.
export module test.host

import (
  nanyx/async as (Async)
  nanyx/host.runtime
  nanyx/random as (Random)
  nanyx.cli/console as (Console)
  nanyx.cli/env as (Env)
  nanyx.fs/filesystem as (FileSystem)
  nanyx.test.host/test.runs as (isIo, isolated, seededTest)
  nanyx.wasi/preview1.system as wasiSystem
)

--| Runs the test the runtime asks for: `entry`, the test build's entry point, given the test's index,
  with `Async` and a `Random` seeded with the runtime's seed. A test marked `@test(#io)` runs with the console
  host's real `Console`, `Env` and `FileSystem` (see `nanyx.wasi`'s `preview1.system`); any other,
  with a `Console` whose output goes to the runtime and whose input is empty (see `test.runs`). What
  `entry` returns is ignored.
export def run
  : [HostRuntime, Async] ([Async, Console, Env, FileSystem, Random] int -> a) -> ()
  = { entry ->
    if isIo() then wasiSystem.provide({ _ -> seededTest(entry) }) else isolated(entry)
  }
